  "Id": 167,
  "Title": "ISMC hosts a sell-out evening at 2015 Nour Festival",
  "NewsSubTitle": null,
  "NewsSummary": "ISMC's talk on Islamic parks and gardens",
  "GlobalNews": false,
  "AlternateNewsTitle": null,
  "AlternateNewsSubtitle": null,
  "AlternateNewsSummary": null,
  "NewsContent": "<p>The UK may be under a grey cloud until March, but this week saw Londoner's attention turn to the sunny skies of Morocco, East Africa and Egypt as ISMC hosted a sell-out evening as part of the 2015 </p><p>Nour Festival of Arts.  </p><p>Over 70 people gathered at Kensington's Leighton House to hear three renowned experts speak on Islamic parks and gardens as vehicles of social change. </p><p>The evening's Chair, Philip Wood, introduced fascinating presentations from Jurjen van der Tas: Deputy Director of the Historic Cities Program of the Aga Khan Trust for Culture, Tom Stuart-Smith: </p><p>Internationally-renowned landscape gardener and RHS Chelsea Gold medallist and Mark Bryant: Development Officer for the Centre for the Study of Islam, University of Cardiff.  </p><p>The ensuing discussion was wide-ranging and included thoughts on how to usefully include traditional Islamic water features in London gardens, to ideas on how to maintain the beauty and tranquility of Al-Azhar Park in Cairo.</p>",
